- `test('create then get',async()=>{const c=await callTool('note_create',{title:'A',body:'B'});const id=c.content[0].text.match(/n\d+/)[0];const g=await callTool('note_get',{note_id:id});assert.match(g.content[0].text,/A/)})`
  - So `note_create` returns `{content:[{text:...}]}` where text contains something matching `n\d+` (the id like `n123`)
  - `note_get` returns `{content:[{text:...}]}` where text matches `/A/` (the title)
- `test('lists two tools',async()=>assert.equal((await listTools()).length,2))`
  - listTools returns array of length 2

Let me think about the id format. The regex is `/n\d+/` so the id must be `n` followed by digits. A stable generated id could be `n` + incrementing counter.

